course: world
lesson: 31
title: The skeleton and muscles
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Inside your body is a frame of bones called the skeleton; an adult has 206 bones.
explain: The skeleton holds you up, gives your body its shape and protects soft parts: the skull guards the brain and the ribs guard the heart and lungs.
explain: Bones are alive: they grow, they mend when they break, and inside many of them soft marrow makes new blood cells.
explain: Where two bones meet there is a joint, such as the knee, the elbow or the shoulder, which lets the body bend and turn.
explain: Muscles move the bones. A muscle can only pull, never push, so muscles work in pairs: when you bend your arm the biceps at the front pulls, and to straighten it the triceps at the back pulls.
explain: Your heart is a muscle too, and it works all your life without you thinking about it.
fact: An adult skeleton has 206 bones.
fact: A baby is born with about 300 bones, and some of them join together as it grows.
fact: The skull protects the brain.
fact: The ribs protect the heart and the lungs.
fact: The backbone (spine) is a column of small bones that holds the body upright and protects the spinal cord, the thick bundle of nerves running down your back.
fact: A joint is the place where two bones meet.
fact: Muscles are joined to bones by tough cords called tendons.
fact: A muscle can only pull; it cannot push.
fact: The biceps bends the arm and the triceps straightens it.
fact: Bone marrow inside many bones makes blood cells.
fact: The longest bone in the body is the thigh bone (femur).
fact: Bones need calcium, from foods like milk, cheese and green vegetables, to stay strong.
exercise: How many bones does an adult skeleton have? | 206 | 26 | 1000 | 52
exercise: Which part of the skeleton protects the brain? | The skull | The ribs | The backbone | The pelvis
exercise: Which bones protect the heart and the lungs? | The ribs | The skull | The thigh bones | The finger bones
exercise: What is a joint? | A place where two bones meet | A very short bone | A muscle that moves a bone | A cord joining muscle to bone
exercise: What can a muscle do on its own? | Pull, but not push | Push, but not pull | Both push and pull | Neither push nor pull
exercise: Which muscle pulls to bend your arm at the elbow? | The biceps | The triceps | The heart | The calf muscle
exercise: What joins muscles to bones? | Tendons | The joints | The nerves | The veins
exercise: What do muscles do to move the body? | They pull on the bones | They push the bones apart | They make the bones longer | They bend the bones themselves
exercise: Which is true about bones? | They are alive and can mend themselves | They are dead like stones | They never grow after birth | They cannot heal once broken
exercise: What is made in the marrow inside many bones? | Blood cells | Saliva | Hair | Food juices
exercise: Which is the longest bone in the human body? | The thigh bone | The backbone | The skull | The upper arm bone
exercise: Which food helps to keep bones strong? | Milk and cheese, rich in calcium | Sweets, rich in sugar | Crisps, rich in salt | Fizzy drinks with lots of gas and bubbles
exercise: Which part of the skeleton keeps the body upright? | The backbone | The ribs | The skull | The collarbone
exercise: Why do a baby's bones number more than an adult's? | Some bones join together as the baby grows | Adults lose bones when they fall | Bones dissolve when we stop growing | Babies have extra bones in their feet only
test: You straighten your arm to reach a book on a shelf. Which muscle is pulling? | The triceps at the back of the arm | The biceps at the front of the arm | No muscle, the arm falls straight | The biceps, by pushing
test: A cyclist wears a helmet. Which bone is the helmet helping to protect? | The skull, which guards the brain | The ribs, which guard the lungs | The backbone, which guards the heart | The thigh bone, which holds blood
test: Which is true? | Muscles work in pairs because each can only pull | One muscle both bends and straightens a joint | Muscles push bones to straighten them | Bones move by themselves without muscles
test: What would happen if we had no joints at all? | We could not bend our arms, legs or back | We would bend more easily | Our muscles would become stronger | Nothing, joints are only for decoration
test: A girl broke her wrist, and after some weeks in a cast it was whole again. Why? | Living bone grew and joined the break | The cast glued the bone together | A new bone grew in another place | The muscle took over the job of the bone
test: Which of these is a joint? | The knee | The skull | The thigh bone | The biceps
test: Which statement about the heart is true? | It is a muscle that works without us thinking | It is a bone inside the ribs | It only works when we tell it to and think about it | It is a joint between the lungs
test: Granny is told to eat more yoghurt and cheese. What are these foods mostly helping? | Her bones, by giving them calcium | Her eyes, by giving them light | Her hair, by making it curly | Her lungs, by giving them air
test: What does the spinal cord have around it for protection? | The bones of the backbone | The curved ribs of the chest | The hard bones of the skull | The two long thigh bones of the legs
test: wait how come u can bend ur knee but not ur shin in the middle | The knee is a joint where two bones meet; the shin has no joint in its middle | The shin bone is too short to bend | Muscles in the shin push it straight | The knee has no bones inside it
test: my arm muscle pulls it bent, so what straightens it back, does it just push? | Another muscle at the back, the triceps, pulls it straight | The biceps pushes the arm back straight | The bone springs back by itself | The elbow joint pushes it straight
test: Leo says bones are dead, like rocks inside you. true? | No, bones are alive: they grow, mend and make blood cells | Yes, bones are dead and only hold you up | Yes, but they come alive when they break | No, bones are made of muscle that has hardened
test: my big sis reckons a baby has fewer bones than us cos it's tiny. right? | No, a baby has about 300 and some join together later | Yes, a baby has about 100 and grows more | Yes, a baby has no bones until it can walk | No, a baby has exactly 206 like an adult
exam: State the number of bones in the skeleton of an adult human being. | Two hundred and six | Three hundred | One hundred and six | Two hundred and sixty
exam: Identify the organs protected by the ribs. | The heart and the lungs | The brain and the eyes | The stomach and the intestines | The kidneys and the liver
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the action of a muscle? | A muscle is able only to pull, never to push | A muscle is able only to push, never to pull | A muscle is able both to push and to pull | A muscle neither pushes nor pulls
exam: Identify the longest bone of the human body. | The thigh bone, or femur | The backbone, or the spine | The upper arm bone, or humerus | The shin bone, or the tibia
